WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.710
New York City.

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:02.710
The Big Apple.

00:00:02.710 --> 00:00:29.679
It isn't the capital of the United States,
but it's still the country's beating heart.

00:00:29.679 --> 00:00:34.760
It's wild, weird, and wonderful, and in order
to truly understand how it turned into the

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:39.590
metropolis it is today, you'll have to take
a look at one of the most influential figures

00:00:39.590 --> 00:00:41.070
in the city's history.

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:47.220
It's a tale of corruption, fraud, bribery,
ostentatious wealth, a little bit of creative

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:52.050
legal interpretation, and a lot of sheer audacity
and willpower.

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:57.011
One of the most important figures of 19th
century New York was William Magear Tweed,

00:00:57.011 --> 00:01:02.260
also known as "Boss" Tweed and the Tiger of
Tammany, for reasons that will soon become

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:03.610
clear.

00:01:03.610 --> 00:01:07.580
On one hand, Tweed was responsible for building
some of the infrastructure that made it possible

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:08.900
for the city to grow.

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:13.350
He improved the water and sewer systems, built
city streets, and oversaw the construction

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:17.660
of major landmarks, like the Metropolitan
Museum of Art, Central Park, the Brooklyn

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:20.990
Bridge, and the American Museum of Natural
History.

00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:25.580
On the other hand, he was one of the most
corrupt politicians ever to rise to power

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:27.110
in the US.

00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:31.840
By the time he died in 1878, it was estimated
that he and his network of cronies had stolen

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:39.070
millions from the state — a sum that's estimated
to be as much as $3.5 billion in today's money.

00:01:39.070 --> 00:01:40.070
How?

00:01:40.070 --> 00:01:44.140
That's at the heart of our story today.

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:49.000
Title: Street-fighting firemen

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:53.940
Tweed was born on April 3, 1823, and according
to most accounts, he had an average childhood.

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:57.990
He had the basic sort of education typically
available to children growing up in Manhattan

00:01:57.990 --> 00:02:01.870
at the time; he once completed a brief stint
as a chairmaker's apprentice.

00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:06.250
Tweed’s life seemed to take a dramatic shift
when, as a teenager, he attached himself to

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:08.170
his local volunteer fire company.

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:13.010
Here's where a bit of a secondary history
lesson is needed to truly do the story justice.

00:02:13.010 --> 00:02:16.760
The volunteer fire fighting companies of the
mid-19th century were nothing like the ones

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:17.760
of today.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:22.030
As a fairly new idea, volunteer companies
and they were half-gang, half fraternity,

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:25.730
with only a small amount of effort reserved
for actual firefighting.

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:30.840
Companies inspired strong allegiances among
their members and were closely tied to the

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:32.550
local politics of their area.

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:36.780
It wasn't unusual to have more than one company
racing to put out a fire, only to get distracted

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:41.770
by the sight of a rival company and have the
entire thing dissolve into a massive street

00:02:41.770 --> 00:02:42.770
brawl.

00:02:42.770 --> 00:02:46.840
And even as a teen, Tweed had quite the reputation
as one of his company's best brawlers.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:52.349
Tweed had attached himself to Engine Company
33, which was also known as Black Joke.

00:02:52.349 --> 00:02:56.510
For an idea of just what sort of folks this
particular engine company attracted, let's

00:02:56.510 --> 00:02:59.110
jump ahead a few decades for just a moment.

00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:04.370
In 1863, New York was torn apart by a series
of draft riots that led to more than 100 deaths

00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:08.069
and somewhere around $1.5 million in damages.

00:03:08.069 --> 00:03:12.599
Leading the way were the men of the Black
Joke, angry that firefighters weren't exempt

00:03:12.599 --> 00:03:16.890
from the draft that was sending able-bodied
men from across the city to fight in the Civil

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:17.890
War.

00:03:17.890 --> 00:03:22.170
They started by burning the city's draft office,
and it kicked off a full-scale riot that descended

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:26.240
into race riots so bad that the fire company
retreated from the front lines and went to

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:30.910
defend their own neighborhood from the burning,
pillaging, and beatings.

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:36.430
That is the company Tweed joined as a teen,
where he first made a name for himself.

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:40.620
After establishing himself as a charismatic
yet street-smart figure in the neighborhood,

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:45.080
he kicked off his political campaign and secured
a position as alderman of the Seventh Ward.

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:49.629
(An alderman is a judicial official that oversaw
some minor civil and criminal cases, and worked

00:03:49.629 --> 00:03:50.890
closely with the mayor.)

00:03:50.890 --> 00:03:52.860
That was in 1852.

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:54.940
Tweed rose through the political ranks fast.

00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:57.490
By 1853, he had a seat in the House of Representatives.

00:03:57.490 --> 00:04:02.190
Life in Washington, DC didn't suit him, however,
and he headed back to the city after just

00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:03.260
a single term.

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:08.060
He'd kept his finger firmly on the pulse of
the city's political scene, and in 1856, he

00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:11.370
landed a position on the city's new board
of supervisors.

00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:16.599
From there, he didn't look back.

00:04:16.599 --> 00:04:19.989
TITLE: Taking over at Tammany Hall

00:04:19.989 --> 00:04:24.270
Now, a little background about one of the
organizations that shaped New York City through

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:25.319
the 1800s.

00:04:25.319 --> 00:04:29.539
Tammany Hall was founded in the 1780s, and
it was originally meant to be a fraternal

00:04:29.539 --> 00:04:34.969
society where like-minded individuals could
get together, socialize, and talk about politics.

00:04:34.969 --> 00:04:38.860
Their actions became increasingly political
as the decades went on, and by the 1840s,

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:43.139
they had firmly established themselves as
an arm of the Democratic party.

00:04:43.139 --> 00:04:46.749
Even more importantly, they had developed
a deep connection with the city's ever-growing

00:04:46.749 --> 00:04:49.319
population of immigrants -- particularly the
Irish.

00:04:49.319 --> 00:04:54.069
Tammany Hall helped countless new arrivals
get jobs, set up lives, and even get achieve

00:04:54.069 --> 00:04:55.069
US citizenship.

00:04:55.069 --> 00:04:59.490
In turn, Tammany Hall got votes for the candidates
they put forward in elections across the city

00:04:59.490 --> 00:05:00.580
and state.

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:03.340
It seemed to work out for everyone.

00:05:03.340 --> 00:05:06.270
Tammany Hall always had the stink of corruption
about it.

00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:10.080
As early as the 1850s, it was clear that those
who were capable of rising to power within

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:14.479
the organization were also the type to color
outside the lines.

00:05:14.479 --> 00:05:18.440
Isaac Fowler was the Grand Sachem — in other
words, the boss — before Tweed stepped into

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:23.169
the position in 1868, and his term ended when
he was charged with embezzlement and fled

00:05:23.169 --> 00:05:24.169
to Mexico.

00:05:24.169 --> 00:05:28.469
In spite of the scandal, the political machine
that was Tammany Hall kept increasing their

00:05:28.469 --> 00:05:32.339
influence over the city -- this time with
Boss Tweed at the helm.

00:05:32.339 --> 00:05:35.659
Tweed spend most of the decade establishing
himself in the neighborhood, where he was

00:05:35.659 --> 00:05:38.360
already known as a larger-than-life character
-- literally.

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:43.249
At a time when the average height for a man
was around 5-foot-6, Tweed towered over most

00:05:43.249 --> 00:05:45.419
at an impressive height of about six feet.

00:05:45.419 --> 00:05:49.120
He topped the scales at almost 300 pounds,
too, at a time when the average weight was

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:50.669
closer to 150.

00:05:50.669 --> 00:05:55.800
Add in the 10-and-a-half carat diamond he
liked to wear all the time, and you had an

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:57.599
imposing figure, indeed.

00:05:57.599 --> 00:06:01.979
It was easy for such a memorable man to be
the center of attention, and that was a significant

00:06:01.979 --> 00:06:04.050
part of why Tweed was so successful.

00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:07.849
He wasn't just a politician sitting up on
high, raking in the cash.

00:06:07.849 --> 00:06:12.499
He was on the streets, shaking hands, handing
out cigars and whiskey, and -- perhaps most

00:06:12.499 --> 00:06:15.770
importantly -- listening to his constituents.

00:06:15.770 --> 00:06:20.350
Tweed was famous for remembering names and
faces, investing himself in the stories that

00:06:20.350 --> 00:06:23.389
people had to tell, and ultimately, doing
them favors.

00:06:23.389 --> 00:06:28.669
True, those favors often had another attached
in return, but as Tweed was climbing the political

00:06:28.669 --> 00:06:32.189
ladder, he had an impressively modest reputation
for honesty.

00:06:32.189 --> 00:06:38.039
TITLE: Tweed, immigration, and voter fraud

00:06:38.039 --> 00:06:42.639
Many of the positions that Tweed and his associates
held were still elected positions, and that

00:06:42.639 --> 00:06:47.619
brings the story around to another important
point: just how did Tweed’s squad manage

00:06:47.619 --> 00:06:49.509
to get themselves elected so regularly?

00:06:49.509 --> 00:06:53.909
A big part of their success was the fact that
the ever-growing population of Irish immigrants

00:06:53.909 --> 00:06:57.580
saw them as an organization that truly cared
about their welfare.

00:06:57.580 --> 00:07:01.750
Many had fled The Great Famine, a catastrophic
crop failure in Ireland that led to the deaths

00:07:01.750 --> 00:07:03.849
of somewhere around a million people.

00:07:03.849 --> 00:07:07.559
When starving families of survivors reached
New York, Tammany Hall welcomed them with

00:07:07.559 --> 00:07:08.559
open arms.

00:07:08.559 --> 00:07:12.770
They even helped them out with baskets of
food and coal for their fires.

00:07:12.770 --> 00:07:14.869
But it wasn't all good will.

00:07:14.869 --> 00:07:19.439
Tweed was notorious for a brazen scheme where
he would recruit a few dozen volunteers from

00:07:19.439 --> 00:07:23.780
among their loyal followers, ply them with
alcohol, and register them in multiple polling

00:07:23.780 --> 00:07:24.780
places.

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:28.439
Then, he would pay law enforcement officers
to escort them from one polling place to the

00:07:28.439 --> 00:07:31.149
next, and to make sure they cast their votes.

00:07:31.149 --> 00:07:34.770
One person would vote dozens of times, and
that all added up to votes for Tammany Hall.

00:07:34.770 --> 00:07:36.860
None of this was a secret.

00:07:36.860 --> 00:07:41.119
Tweed also paid the officers to arrest any
voting inspector that started to get suspicious.

00:07:41.119 --> 00:07:44.919
The most flagrant instance of Tweed using
his clout and influence with immigrants to

00:07:44.919 --> 00:07:47.849
get Tammany Hall allies more votes came in
1868.

00:07:47.849 --> 00:07:52.709
That's the year that Tweed arranged to have
around 44,000 immigrants become naturalized

00:07:52.709 --> 00:07:55.199
citizens in the weeks before a major election.

00:07:55.199 --> 00:07:59.330
It didn't go unnoticed, either; when Tammany
Hall members were elected to offices at the

00:07:59.330 --> 00:08:03.389
mayoral level, and all the way up to governor
of New York, the House of Representatives

00:08:03.389 --> 00:08:08.349
put together a committee to determine the
extent of Tweed's interference with the elections.

00:08:08.349 --> 00:08:10.449
Nothing came of it.

00:08:10.449 --> 00:08:14.830
The so-called Tweed ring — only continued
to increase their power and influence.

00:08:14.830 --> 00:08:19.249
The same year he was under investigation for
interfering with elections, Tweed was named

00:08:19.249 --> 00:08:23.459
to the head of Tammany Hall and elected to
the New York Senate.

00:08:23.459 --> 00:08:25.229
Those were just stepping stones, though.

00:08:25.229 --> 00:08:29.379
While Tweed was serving as House Rep at the
federal level, he had made friends across

00:08:29.379 --> 00:08:30.400
party lines.

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:35.070
That allowed him to pass a charter shifting
the responsibilities of local governance — particularly

00:08:35.070 --> 00:08:38.880
those that concerned budget and oversight
— out of the hands of federally appointed

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:42.500
boards and into the control of locally appointed
authorities.

00:08:42.500 --> 00:08:45.130
And that was the truly brilliant part.

00:08:45.130 --> 00:08:49.130
Remember when we mentioned that Washington,
DC hadn't been to Tweed's liking, and he opted

00:08:49.130 --> 00:08:52.270
to leave federal politics to head back to
New York City?

00:08:52.270 --> 00:08:56.950
When he did, that very same city charter was
waiting for him, and it passed complete control

00:08:56.950 --> 00:08:59.330
of the treasury into his direct authority.

00:08:59.330 --> 00:09:04.630
He and his cronies now had absolute control
over what the treasury paid out, and they

00:09:04.630 --> 00:09:43.860
took advantage of it in every way they could.

00:09:43.860 --> 00:10:32.340
TITLE: The Tweed Courthouse

00:10:32.340 --> 00:10:36.860
No building or enterprise illustrates the
extent of the Tweed Ring's corruption better

00:10:36.860 --> 00:10:39.490
than the Old New York County Courthouse.

00:10:39.490 --> 00:10:44.310
The building is a stunning display of 19th
century architecture and craftsmanship, and

00:10:44.310 --> 00:10:48.340
it's also known as one of the most prominent
legacies of Tweed reign at Tammany Hall.

00:10:48.340 --> 00:10:52.351
It also ended up being the most expensive
building ever built in the United States at

00:10:52.351 --> 00:10:55.880
the time, costing even more than the US Capitol
Building, for the simple reason that Tweed

00:10:55.880 --> 00:11:00.530
and his cronies were in charge of both issuing
the bills and paying them from the city's

00:11:00.530 --> 00:11:01.530
coffers.

00:11:01.530 --> 00:11:05.100
The Tweed Ring was unthinkably audacious when
it came to manipulating these construction

00:11:05.100 --> 00:11:06.100
bills.

00:11:06.100 --> 00:11:10.250
The original budget for the entire building
was $250,000, a hefty sum for the time.

00:11:10.250 --> 00:11:15.230
To give an idea of just how much the courthouse
ended up costing, that original budget was

00:11:15.230 --> 00:11:20.230
only enough to pay the bill for the purchase
of the courthouse's brooms.

00:11:20.230 --> 00:11:21.840
Other items were just as costly.

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:26.370
Plumbing set the city back more than $1 million,
the thermometers ended up at a rather pricey

00:11:26.370 --> 00:11:35.620
$7,500, and a set of three tables and four
chairs came to a grand total of $179,729.

00:11:35.620 --> 00:11:37.950
And there were a lot more of these “chairs.”

00:11:37.950 --> 00:11:42.230
For the amount of chair money that Tweed actually
billed the city for, they could have bought

00:11:42.230 --> 00:11:46.420
around 315,000 of them — and that's somewhere
around 17 miles of chairs.

00:11:46.420 --> 00:11:52.340
Carpets for the courthouse cost around $5
million, largely because there was enough

00:11:52.340 --> 00:11:56.220
carpet ordered to cover City Hall Park three
times.

00:11:56.220 --> 00:11:58.100
What did they do with all that carpet?

00:11:58.100 --> 00:12:01.670
Nothing, because it was ordered from companies
that didn't exist.

00:12:01.670 --> 00:12:02.990
Some companies did exist.

00:12:02.990 --> 00:12:06.120
Before construction started, Tweed had conveniently
bought a quarry.

00:12:06.120 --> 00:12:10.100
It was that quarry that was used to source
all the marble for the courthouse, and that

00:12:10.100 --> 00:12:14.760
bill alone was higher than the entire cost
of building another courthouse in Brooklyn.

00:12:14.760 --> 00:12:18.320
Not all of the money was going right into
Tweed's pockets, as he also made sure his

00:12:18.320 --> 00:12:20.740
fellow officers at Tammany Hall were well
cared for.

00:12:20.740 --> 00:12:24.940
The grand marshal at the time was Andrew J.
Garvey, and he earned the nickname "Prince

00:12:24.940 --> 00:12:29.810
of Plasterers" after he ended up pocketing
around $3 million for the work he did on the

00:12:29.810 --> 00:12:32.580
courthouse, and for subsequent repairs to
his original labor.

00:12:32.580 --> 00:12:34.810
So, how much did it end up costing?

00:12:34.810 --> 00:12:39.630
When the state legislature approved the build
for $250,000, that was the equivalent of around

00:12:39.630 --> 00:12:40.960
$6 million in today's money.

00:12:40.960 --> 00:12:44.720
When it was finally finished, the bill hit
$12 million.

00:12:44.720 --> 00:12:49.860
Adjusted for inflation, that's about the equivalent
of $200 million today.

00:12:49.860 --> 00:12:55.730
It's no wonder that people started catching
on.

00:12:55.730 --> 00:12:59.320
TITLE: The power of editorial cartoons

00:12:59.320 --> 00:13:02.080
While there were plenty of people willing
to look the other way when it came to Boss

00:13:02.080 --> 00:13:06.180
Tweed's criminal ways, not everyone was so
willing to allow the Tweed Ring to keep running

00:13:06.180 --> 00:13:07.520
riot across the city.

00:13:07.520 --> 00:13:12.260
One of those determined to bring down the
corruption of Tammany Hall was Thomas Nast.

00:13:12.260 --> 00:13:15.720
Nast was a German immigrant who settled in
New York City and discovered very early in

00:13:15.720 --> 00:13:18.030
life that he had a natural artistic talent.

00:13:18.030 --> 00:13:21.540
The timing was excellent; with the start of
the Civil War, Americans were growing increasingly

00:13:21.540 --> 00:13:26.170
interested in what was going on in other parts
of the country and, specifically, in the war.

00:13:26.170 --> 00:13:29.900
Newspapers were still the way many people
got their information, and Nast's drawings

00:13:29.900 --> 00:13:33.750
of some of the most famous battles of the
war catapulted him to fame.

00:13:33.750 --> 00:13:38.740
He was one of Abraham Lincoln's favorite propagandists,
and he took on the big issues of slavery,

00:13:38.740 --> 00:13:40.870
war, and eventually, political corruption.

00:13:40.870 --> 00:13:44.620
That was a bigger deal than you might realize,
because his illustrations reached an entire

00:13:44.620 --> 00:13:48.680
group of people who otherwise wouldn't have
cared much for what was in the paper.

00:13:48.680 --> 00:13:50.760
Those people were illiterate.

00:13:50.760 --> 00:13:54.060
Nast was working at Harper's Weekly when Tweed
was running Tammany Hall.

00:13:54.060 --> 00:13:58.340
His campaign to shine a spotlight directly
on the rampant political corruption that dominated

00:13:58.340 --> 00:14:03.070
the institution started by his calling them
on voter fraud, and it only escalated from

00:14:03.070 --> 00:14:04.070
there.

00:14:04.070 --> 00:14:07.130
Early on, Nast — along with reporters at
the New York Times — struggled to get something

00:14:07.130 --> 00:14:08.850
to stick to the Tweed Ring.

00:14:08.850 --> 00:14:13.540
Despite the fact that everyone knew what was
going on, uncovering concrete evidence proved

00:14:13.540 --> 00:14:14.540
difficult.

00:14:14.540 --> 00:14:16.870
...Until 1871, that is..

00:14:16.870 --> 00:14:21.310
While Tweed had gone above and beyond to keep
his constituents on his good side, Nast was

00:14:21.310 --> 00:14:23.300
not so easily swayed.

00:14:23.300 --> 00:14:27.410
It's also important to note that Nast’s
campaign wasn't out of any righteous indignation

00:14:27.410 --> 00:14:31.350
or a determination to clean up the city, no
matter what the cost.

00:14:31.350 --> 00:14:36.560
Nast was a rabid devotee of the Republican
Party, and as such, he staunchly opposed the

00:14:36.560 --> 00:14:40.170
idea that the Irish working class immigrants
were anything but trouble.

00:14:40.170 --> 00:14:44.650
Since Tweed was so closely allied with that
portion of New York City's population, he

00:14:44.650 --> 00:14:46.060
clearly had to go.

00:14:46.060 --> 00:14:50.510
And on July 18, 1871, Nast and his colleagues
at the Times were given a gift.

00:14:50.510 --> 00:14:55.090
One of Tweed's associates was a man named
Jimmy O'Brien, and O'Brien believed he wasn't

00:14:55.090 --> 00:14:59.170
being treated fairly, in the grand scheme
of things, so he did something that had to

00:14:59.170 --> 00:15:04.270
have taken an insane amount of courage -- he
took duplicates of the city's ledgers, strolled

00:15:04.270 --> 00:15:07.830
into the Times, and handed them to an editor
named Louis Jennings.

00:15:07.830 --> 00:15:10.130
Then, he walked away.

00:15:10.130 --> 00:15:14.460
The Times began running all the sordid details
of Tweed's operation, and that's when Nast

00:15:14.460 --> 00:15:16.290
really swung into high gear.

00:15:16.290 --> 00:15:20.210
Until this moment of reckoning, when they
were handed actual proof and concrete numbers,

00:15:20.210 --> 00:15:22.620
his cartoons were mere personal attacks.

00:15:22.620 --> 00:15:26.570
He couldn't prove anything aside from the
fact that he was wealthy, but making fun of

00:15:26.570 --> 00:15:28.550
that had no real effect.

00:15:28.550 --> 00:15:32.960
But when Nast started drawing cartoons showing
Tweed as a fallen emperor, as passing the

00:15:32.960 --> 00:15:37.370
blame onto others, and as seeing himself as
too powerful to be contained by a prison,

00:15:37.370 --> 00:15:42.460
it was illustrating a more abstract situation
in a way that everyone could understand.

00:15:42.460 --> 00:15:48.520
And it was Boss Tweed himself who best summed
up the problem he now had: "I don't care a

00:15:48.520 --> 00:15:50.230
straw for your newspaper articles.

00:15:50.230 --> 00:15:54.810
My constituents don't know how to read, but
they can't help seeing them damned pictures."

00:15:54.810 --> 00:15:58.870
It was all downhill from there.

00:15:58.870 --> 00:16:03.680
TITLE: The fall

00:16:03.680 --> 00:16:07.520
Things started happening fast after O'Brien
handed over proof of just what Tweed and his

00:16:07.520 --> 00:16:09.110
cronies had done to the city.

00:16:09.110 --> 00:16:12.770
Members of the Tweed Ring started to flee
the country ahead of the fallout they knew

00:16:12.770 --> 00:16:16.200
was now inevitable, and Tweed himself was
arrested in October.

00:16:16.200 --> 00:16:20.551
Surprisingly, he was released on bail and
allowed to run in a local election, and even

00:16:20.551 --> 00:16:22.839
more surprisingly, he still won.

00:16:22.839 --> 00:16:28.520
But the end was nigh, and the following few
years would be marred by a series of trials

00:16:28.520 --> 00:16:32.120
appropriately held in the still-unfinished
Tweed Courthouse.

00:16:32.120 --> 00:16:39.740
It wasn't until 1873 that Tweed was found
guilty of 204 of the 220 charges brought against

00:16:39.740 --> 00:16:40.740
him.

00:16:40.740 --> 00:16:44.100
He was sentenced to serve 12-year jail term
that was quickly reduced; he was also ordered

00:16:44.100 --> 00:16:46.220
to pay a $12,750 fine.

00:16:46.220 --> 00:16:50.550
Even at the same time Tweed lost his freedom,
Tammany Hall lost their boss.

00:16:50.550 --> 00:16:51.990
But it wasn't the end for them.

00:16:51.990 --> 00:16:55.080
They passed their leadership into the hands
of a man named John Kelly.

00:16:55.080 --> 00:16:59.200
He made major strides in overhauling Tammany
Hall and its image, turning it into something

00:16:59.200 --> 00:17:03.350
more in line with what anti-corruption campaigners
and reformers had in mind.

00:17:03.350 --> 00:17:04.839
It didn't last long, though.

00:17:04.839 --> 00:17:08.589
Voter fraud came back with the very next leader,
who took over in 1888.

00:17:08.589 --> 00:17:11.770
By the beginning of the 20th century, there
was another group that had a vested interest

00:17:11.770 --> 00:17:13.839
in Tammany Hall: the mob.

00:17:13.839 --> 00:17:18.439
When it came time for the 1932 presidential
elections, the Tammany Hall delegates were

00:17:18.439 --> 00:17:23.389
escorted to the Democratic National Convention
by the likes of Meyer Lansky, Lucky Luciano,

00:17:23.389 --> 00:17:24.389
and Frank Costello.

00:17:24.389 --> 00:17:28.429
Afterwards, Franklin Delano Roosevelt delivered
some major blows to the all-encompassing power

00:17:28.429 --> 00:17:33.499
of Tammany Hall — in part, by restricting
immigration and limiting their core supporters.

00:17:33.499 --> 00:17:37.570
Things were never quite the same, and after
a brief scramble for power in the 1950s, Tammany

00:17:37.570 --> 00:17:44.119
Hall disappeared in the mid-1960s, its doors
closed and its properties sent to auction.

00:17:44.119 --> 00:17:47.809
But now, back to Tweed as his story isn't
quite over yet.

00:17:47.809 --> 00:17:51.279
It turned out that even his jail time wasn't
much of a punishment.

00:17:51.279 --> 00:17:55.409
Tweed served his time in a private room in
Blackwell's Island, with a window looking

00:17:55.409 --> 00:17:56.620
out over his domain.

00:17:56.620 --> 00:17:59.790
When his year was up, he was brought up again
on civil charges.

00:17:59.790 --> 00:18:03.450
Prosecutors were hoping to recover some of
the money that he had funneled out of the

00:18:03.450 --> 00:18:06.980
city's coffers, and when he was unable to
make bail this time, he was confined to the

00:18:06.980 --> 00:18:08.110
Ludlow Street Jail.

00:18:08.110 --> 00:18:10.880
There is, of course, a "but".

00:18:10.880 --> 00:18:14.669
Tweed's cell was a two-bedroom set of rooms
with a small library and his own personal

00:18:14.669 --> 00:18:15.669
aide.

00:18:15.669 --> 00:18:19.039
He talked the warden into letting him have
regular visitors; he was even allowed to head

00:18:19.039 --> 00:18:22.409
out into Central Park for the occasional carriage
ride.

00:18:22.409 --> 00:18:26.249
On December 4, 1875, he headed to his home
with an escort of two guards.

00:18:26.249 --> 00:18:30.039
After telling them he was going to check on
his sick wife, he strolled out the front door,

00:18:30.039 --> 00:18:33.830
crossed the Hudson River in a carriage, and
escaped to New Jersey, where he went on the

00:18:33.830 --> 00:18:36.289
run under the alias "John Secor."

00:18:36.289 --> 00:18:40.249
In the meantime, the courts had continued
without him and ruled that, yes, he did owe

00:18:40.249 --> 00:18:41.879
the city $6 million dollars.

00:18:41.879 --> 00:18:46.450
With nothing really left to lose, Tweed continued
life on the lam, hopping on a schooner and

00:18:46.450 --> 00:18:48.249
sailing down the Atlantic coast.

00:18:48.249 --> 00:18:50.960
He met up with a man who was going by the
name of William Hunt.

00:18:50.960 --> 00:18:55.220
It's not entirely clear who Hunt was, but
we do know that the duo hopped a fishing boat

00:18:55.220 --> 00:18:56.220
to Cuba.

00:18:56.220 --> 00:18:59.190
They were immediately stopped for not having
the proper paperwork, but before the governments

00:18:59.190 --> 00:19:03.649
could coordinate to arrest him, he jumped
on yet another boat bound for Spain.

00:19:03.649 --> 00:19:07.580
Unfortunately for Tweed, the six-week crossing
gave authorities more than enough time to

00:19:07.580 --> 00:19:11.350
send word to Spain that they had a fugitive
headed for their shores.

00:19:11.350 --> 00:19:14.130
Spanish officials were waiting on the dock
for him when he got there.

00:19:14.130 --> 00:19:19.860
There's one last little footnote to the story
that adds to the sheer outrageousness of the

00:19:19.860 --> 00:19:24.169
tale -- the authorities weren't sure who they
were looking for, but they were able to identify

00:19:24.169 --> 00:19:26.889
Tweed based on one of Nast's cartoons.

00:19:26.889 --> 00:19:31.899
It was, appropriately, one that depicted him
in the striped outfit of a convict.

00:19:31.899 --> 00:19:34.830
Hunt was released, but Tweed was shipped back
to the US.

00:19:34.830 --> 00:19:39.110
Only 11 months after escaping, he was back
in his old Ludlow Street cell.

00:19:39.110 --> 00:19:43.539
He was 100 pounds lighter with hair that had
gone completely white.

00:19:43.539 --> 00:19:44.950
He was a broken man.

00:19:44.950 --> 00:19:49.320
Tweed finally agreed to confess to everything
he'd done in exchange for his freedom, and

00:19:49.320 --> 00:19:52.980
in 1877 he went before a special committee
and not only admitted just how much money

00:19:52.980 --> 00:19:55.659
he'd stolen, but he also named names.

00:19:55.659 --> 00:19:59.900
The list was long, and included anyone who
had ever taken bribes to look the other way

00:19:59.900 --> 00:20:03.999
for those who had helped with his election
fraud, or those who had taken kickbacks on

00:20:03.999 --> 00:20:05.590
his construction projects.

00:20:05.590 --> 00:20:09.309
In the end, the governor and attorney general
thanked Tweed for his cooperation, withdrew

00:20:09.309 --> 00:20:12.909
their promise to set him free, and sent him
back to Ludlow.

00:20:12.909 --> 00:20:16.619
Two weeks later, on April 27, 1878, he died.

00:20:16.619 --> 00:20:21.580
He was buried in Green-Wood Cemetery, and
he left New York City a place entirely different

00:20:21.580 --> 00:20:35.169
from the one where he had been born.

